L wrote:
> One of the many things I covet about Linley's Dungeon Crawl is the fact
> that certain dungeon levels have specially coloured wall and floor
> glyphs. Well, now I've added something like that to NetHack.
> << http://www.greyfire.org/~l/glyphcolor.diff >>
>
> Now, instead of the usual gray:
>
> * Beehive walls and floors are yellow (with honeycomb).

Call me an old conservative, but nethack has a "look" which I like. I
like to think the colours in NH are more symbolic than literal (liches
aren't really purple, winter wolves aren't really blue, dwarves aren't
really red etc.) - they just make it easier for our eyes to distinguish
the different elements on the screen so that we can then construct our
own mental image. Hence it is the duty of the colours and symbols to be
clear. A yellow beehive filled with yellow bees is hard to figure out
at a glance. For cockatrice nests, the phrase "You enter a disgusting
nest!" or whatever it is, is far more evocative than any ASCII
representation could be.

> * The inner walls of the Wizard's tower are magenta, and the floors are
> bright blue (note: not the same as cyan)

Yack. What is he, a Hammers fan? :-)

> * The Fire Plane's floor is red.

This presumably makes it much harder to distinguish ground from lava.
It seems like brown, or even black, would be a more "realistic" colour
for the rock surrounding lava. Shouldn't the floor on the plane of
Earth also be coloured brown?

> * The Mines' walls are brown, except for room walls. (Note: only the
> shops and temples in Grotto Town and Bustling Town are actually defined
> as rooms. The rest of the Minetowns, as well as randomly generated
> Minetowns, should work fine.)

Perhaps the patch could "fix" this in the definitions of Grotto and
Bustling town? Otherwise it's going to look odd, isn't it?

> * The Astral Plane's walls are white.

I like this. Subtle, but suitably ennobling.

> * Altars are white, gray, black or red depending on alignment, except
> for Astral altars, which are bright magenta. I'm told this was or is a
> feature in Slash'EM.

I don't like this. Won't the black altars turn out blue for people
playing on a black background (which I believe is most of us, right?)?

